1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,964
You have two graphics cards. Each one

2
00:00:01,964 --> 00:00:04,560
has sixteen gigabytes of VRAM. You do

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,590
the math. Sixteen plus sixteen is thirty-two.

4
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:09,960
So you download a local AI model that

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:12,725
needs more than sixteen gigabytes of memory. You

6
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:15,056
expect the second card to solve the problem.

7
00:00:15,056 --> 00:00:17,845
Instead, the model refuses to load, part

8
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:19,648
of it lands in system RAM, or

9
00:00:19,648 --> 00:00:21,520
the answer comes out slower than before.

10
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,210
And suddenly, the most obvious equation in

11
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:26,520
your computer becomes the most misleading one.

12
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,130
Today, we're answering a question that could save

13
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:31,498
you from buying the wrong GPU. Can two

14
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:35,453
sixteen-gigabyte cards really replace one thirty-two-gigabyte card?

15
00:00:35,453 --> 00:00:37,099
Can they run a larger model? Can

16
00:00:37,099 --> 00:00:38,926
they make one answer faster? And why

17
00:00:38,926 --> 00:00:41,883
do some dual-GPU builds perform beautifully while

18
00:00:41,883 --> 00:00:44,710
others become a very expensive troubleshooting project?

19
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:47,619
There are no invented token-per-second results in

20
00:00:47,619 --> 00:00:49,277
this video. We're going to follow the

21
00:00:49,277 --> 00:00:51,489
data through the machine and separate three

22
00:00:51,489 --> 00:00:53,780
different goals that people keep mixing together.

23
00:00:54,144 --> 00:00:57,004
First, the rule that changes everything: two

24
00:00:57,004 --> 00:01:00,345
GPUs do not automatically become one GPU.

25
00:01:00,345 --> 00:01:02,389
Each graphics card has its own physical

26
00:01:02,389 --> 00:01:04,769
memory, its own processor, and its own

27
00:01:04,769 --> 00:01:06,340
connection to the rest of the system.

28
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:08,850
Your computer can see thirty-two gigabytes of

29
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:12,155
total installed VRAM across two cards. That

30
00:01:12,155 --> 00:01:14,487
does not mean every application can allocate

31
00:01:14,487 --> 00:01:17,980
one uninterrupted thirty-two-gigabyte block on either card.

32
00:01:18,309 --> 00:01:21,302
Think of two sixteen-seat minibuses. Together they

33
00:01:21,302 --> 00:01:24,319
can transport thirty-two passengers. But you cannot

34
00:01:24,319 --> 00:01:27,275
put twenty passengers inside either minibus without

35
00:01:27,275 --> 00:01:28,680
deciding how to split the group.

36
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,487
The same distinction applies to model weights.

37
00:01:31,487 --> 00:01:33,217
If your application knows how to divide

38
00:01:33,217 --> 00:01:35,958
a model across GPUs, the combined memory

39
00:01:35,958 --> 00:01:38,482
can become useful. If the application only

40
00:01:38,482 --> 00:01:41,294
supports one GPU, the second card may

41
00:01:41,294 --> 00:01:43,390
do absolutely nothing for that model.

42
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:46,165
And even when splitting works, the model file

43
00:01:46,165 --> 00:01:48,785
size is not the total memory requirement.

44
00:01:48,785 --> 00:01:52,030
The runtime also needs working buffers, attention-related

45
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:54,280
state, and room for the active context.

46
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,689
Other processes may already be using some

47
00:01:56,689 --> 00:01:59,478
VRAM. Memory pressure also depends on the

48
00:01:59,478 --> 00:02:01,640
precision and architecture of the model.

49
00:02:01,964 --> 00:02:04,752
So a model that occupies, let's say, twenty-four

50
00:02:04,752 --> 00:02:07,048
gigabytes on disk is not guaranteed to fit

51
00:02:07,048 --> 00:02:10,834
across two sixteen-gigabyte cards just because twenty-four is

52
00:02:10,834 --> 00:02:14,332
less than thirty-two. That's a hypothetical file size,

53
00:02:14,332 --> 00:02:16,560
not a measurement of any particular model.

54
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:19,375
Before shopping, ask a better question. What

55
00:02:19,375 --> 00:02:21,454
exactly are you trying to improve? There

56
00:02:21,454 --> 00:02:23,654
are three different answers: fit a bigger

57
00:02:23,654 --> 00:02:26,262
model, make one answer arrive faster,

58
00:02:26,262 --> 00:02:27,880
or serve more requests at once.

59
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,674
Those three jobs can favor different configurations. Confusing

60
00:02:31,674 --> 00:02:34,230
them is where most bad buying advice begins.

61
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,501
Let's start with fitting a bigger model.

62
00:02:36,501 --> 00:02:39,470
In a supported multi-GPU runtime, one common

63
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:41,153
approach is to divide the model by

64
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:44,120
layers. Imagine a stack of processing stages.

65
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:47,379
The first graphics card holds the early stages.

66
00:02:47,379 --> 00:02:50,011
The second holds the later stages. Your input

67
00:02:50,011 --> 00:02:52,355
passes through the first set of layers. The

68
00:02:52,355 --> 00:02:54,800
intermediate result crosses to the other card.

69
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:57,559
The second card completes the remaining work.

70
00:02:57,559 --> 00:02:59,410
The full model can live across both

71
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:02,126
devices, even though neither device has enough

72
00:03:02,126 --> 00:03:04,040
memory to hold every weight alone.

73
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,208
This is often called layer splitting or

74
00:03:06,208 --> 00:03:08,647
pipeline parallelism. And it is not merely

75
00:03:08,647 --> 00:03:11,111
a theoretical trick. It's an option described

76
00:03:11,111 --> 00:03:14,360
in the official llama.cpp multi-GPU guide.

77
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:17,159
The advantage is straightforward. A model that

78
00:03:17,159 --> 00:03:20,513
would otherwise require substantial CPU offloading

79
00:03:20,513 --> 00:03:21,751
may be able to keep more

80
00:03:21,751 --> 00:03:23,380
of its work on graphics hardware.

81
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:26,256
The catch is equally important. When you generate

82
00:03:26,256 --> 00:03:28,552
a single answer, the next token still has

83
00:03:28,552 --> 00:03:30,067
to make its way through the relevant

84
00:03:30,067 --> 00:03:32,855
model computation. The cards do not magically

85
00:03:32,855 --> 00:03:34,850
finish the same work twice as fast

86
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:36,520
just because you installed two of them.

87
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:38,815
Layer splitting also has its own memory

88
00:03:38,815 --> 00:03:42,385
placement rules. In llama.cpp's layer mode, for

89
00:03:42,385 --> 00:03:45,486
example, a layer's key-value cache is associated

90
00:03:45,486 --> 00:03:47,240
with the GPU holding that layer.

91
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,445
The details vary across runtimes. You cannot

92
00:03:50,445 --> 00:03:53,906
assume every implementation splits weights, cache, and

93
00:03:53,906 --> 00:03:56,310
working buffers identically. And there's a physical

94
00:03:56,310 --> 00:03:59,147
connection between those two cards. The intermediate

95
00:03:59,147 --> 00:04:00,690
results have to move across it.

96
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:03,456
Which brings us to the second approach: dividing

97
00:04:03,456 --> 00:04:06,773
the computation inside the layers themselves. This is

98
00:04:06,773 --> 00:04:10,042
called tensor parallelism. Instead of giving different sets

99
00:04:10,042 --> 00:04:13,191
of layers to different GPUs, the runtime distributes

100
00:04:13,191 --> 00:04:16,380
portions of certain operations across multiple GPUs.

101
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,452
Both devices can work on parts of the

102
00:04:18,452 --> 00:04:21,108
same stage. That sounds like the obvious answer

103
00:04:21,108 --> 00:04:24,330
to making one response faster. Sometimes it helps.

104
00:04:24,330 --> 00:04:26,745
But now the GPUs may have to exchange

105
00:04:26,745 --> 00:04:29,300
and combine results repeatedly throughout the model.

106
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,715
If communication is slow relative to computation,

107
00:04:32,715 --> 00:04:34,626
the extra card stops being a free

108
00:04:34,626 --> 00:04:37,306
speed boost. You spend time coordinating two

109
00:04:37,306 --> 00:04:39,720
processors that could have been working independently.

110
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:42,285
The software matters here. A runtime must

111
00:04:42,285 --> 00:04:45,687
support the model architecture, precision, backend, and

112
00:04:45,687 --> 00:04:47,718
parallel mode you want to use. Some

113
00:04:47,718 --> 00:04:50,110
modes are experimental or have restrictions.

114
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:51,984
A setting that works for one model

115
00:04:51,984 --> 00:04:53,787
is not a promise that every model

116
00:04:53,787 --> 00:04:57,621
will run. The current llama.cpp documentation, for

117
00:04:57,621 --> 00:05:00,073
example, treats its tensor-split path as

118
00:05:00,073 --> 00:05:02,380
more experimental than ordinary layer splitting.

119
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:05,781
It also documents compatibility requirements and cases

120
00:05:05,781 --> 00:05:09,399
where interconnect speed hurts performance. vLLM supports

121
00:05:09,399 --> 00:05:12,320
tensor and pipeline parallel strategies too, but

122
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,240
with different configuration choices and deployment goals.

123
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:17,478
Those details must be checked against the

124
00:05:17,478 --> 00:05:19,954
specific version you're running. The lesson is

125
00:05:19,954 --> 00:05:22,094
not that one splitting method is always

126
00:05:22,094 --> 00:05:24,966
faster. The lesson is that 'two GPUs'

127
00:05:24,966 --> 00:05:27,839
is not a performance specification. The distribution

128
00:05:27,839 --> 00:05:29,790
method is part of the specification.

129
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,998
Now imagine a very different situation. You already

130
00:05:32,998 --> 00:05:35,221
have a local model that fits comfortably on

131
00:05:35,221 --> 00:05:38,190
one sixteen-gigabyte GPU. What happens if you

132
00:05:38,190 --> 00:05:40,293
buy another identical card? You might not

133
00:05:40,293 --> 00:05:41,520
want to split the model at all.

134
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:44,392
You could run separate model instances and send

135
00:05:44,392 --> 00:05:47,841
independent requests to each GPU. One card handles

136
00:05:47,841 --> 00:05:50,954
one group of requests; the other handles another.

137
00:05:50,954 --> 00:05:53,022
That is a form of data parallelism, and

138
00:05:53,022 --> 00:05:54,452
it can be useful when you're serving

139
00:05:54,452 --> 00:05:57,460
multiple users or running many independent jobs.

140
00:05:57,780 --> 00:06:00,311
But be precise about the benefit. Two separate

141
00:06:00,311 --> 00:06:03,941
sixteen-gigabyte replicas do not let one twenty-four-gigabyte

142
00:06:03,941 --> 00:06:06,994
model fit inside a single replica. Each

143
00:06:06,994 --> 00:06:09,010
instance still has its own memory limit.

144
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:10,682
And if you are one person waiting

145
00:06:10,682 --> 00:06:12,425
for a single chat answer, running a

146
00:06:12,425 --> 00:06:14,444
second copy of the same model may

147
00:06:14,444 --> 00:06:15,810
not reduce your wait at all.

148
00:06:16,130 --> 00:06:18,934
This is why impressive server throughput screenshots can

149
00:06:18,934 --> 00:06:22,936
mislead desktop users. Total tokens generated across many

150
00:06:22,936 --> 00:06:25,737
simultaneous requests is a different measurement from the

151
00:06:25,737 --> 00:06:28,100
speed of one response on one screen.

152
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:29,883
Now let's talk about something that gets

153
00:06:29,883 --> 00:06:32,275
almost no attention in those two-card shopping

154
00:06:32,275 --> 00:06:35,617
lists: the motherboard. A second PCI Express

155
00:06:35,617 --> 00:06:38,309
slot may physically look full-size without having

156
00:06:38,309 --> 00:06:40,690
the same electrical connection as the first.

157
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:43,216
Some slots use fewer lanes. Some share

158
00:06:43,216 --> 00:06:45,908
lanes with storage or other devices. Some

159
00:06:45,908 --> 00:06:47,651
run through the chipset rather than using

160
00:06:47,651 --> 00:06:49,300
the same path to the CPU.

161
00:06:49,621 --> 00:06:52,253
The exact configuration depends on the processor,

162
00:06:52,253 --> 00:06:55,450
board, firmware, and which slots are occupied.

163
00:06:55,450 --> 00:06:57,145
You need the motherboard manual, not

164
00:06:57,145 --> 00:06:59,050
a photograph of two long slots.

165
00:06:59,370 --> 00:07:01,756
And even if two GPUs fit mechanically,

166
00:07:01,756 --> 00:07:04,208
their cooling systems might not. Thick cards

167
00:07:04,208 --> 00:07:06,840
can block each other, restrict airflow, or

168
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,610
leave almost no room for cables.

169
00:07:08,930 --> 00:07:11,205
Power matters too. Your power supply must

170
00:07:11,205 --> 00:07:13,104
handle both cards and the rest of

171
00:07:13,104 --> 00:07:15,989
the system. You need suitable connectors, sustained

172
00:07:15,989 --> 00:07:18,560
cooling, and enough room inside the case.

173
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:21,106
More hardware adds failure points and operating

174
00:07:21,106 --> 00:07:24,015
costs. Then there's interconnect speed. Some

175
00:07:24,015 --> 00:07:27,669
supported systems have high-bandwidth GPU-to-GPU links.

176
00:07:27,669 --> 00:07:30,420
Others communicate mainly over PCI Express.

177
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:33,081
Not every modern consumer GPU offers a

178
00:07:33,081 --> 00:07:35,497
direct high-speed bridge, and you should never

179
00:07:35,497 --> 00:07:37,215
assume that two cards have one

180
00:07:37,215 --> 00:07:39,080
because the product pictures look similar.

181
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,840
This does not mean PCI Express is always

182
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,676
too slow. Layer splitting can work well without

183
00:07:44,676 --> 00:07:47,801
the same communication requirements as more tightly coupled

184
00:07:47,801 --> 00:07:50,662
tensor parallelism. The actual effect depends on the

185
00:07:50,662 --> 00:07:53,354
workload and implementation. It means you have to

186
00:07:53,354 --> 00:07:55,680
test the connection your machine really has.

187
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,208
What about mixing an older sixteen-gigabyte GPU with

188
00:07:59,208 --> 00:08:02,646
a newer eight-gigabyte one? In some supported setups,

189
00:08:02,646 --> 00:08:05,242
uneven layer splits are possible. The software

190
00:08:05,242 --> 00:08:06,901
can assign more work to the larger

191
00:08:06,901 --> 00:08:08,690
card and less to the smaller one.

192
00:08:09,010 --> 00:08:10,876
But sixteen plus eight is not a

193
00:08:10,876 --> 00:08:13,389
guarantee of twenty-four gigabytes of usable

194
00:08:13,389 --> 00:08:15,961
model capacity. Each GPU still needs

195
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:18,360
its own buffers and cache allocations.

196
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:21,632
Different cards may have different compute features, memory

197
00:08:21,632 --> 00:08:25,526
bandwidth, backend support, and driver requirements. The slower

198
00:08:25,526 --> 00:08:29,180
device can influence end-to-end performance, particularly if

199
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:31,440
your chosen distribution forces work through it.

200
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:34,856
A runtime that handles two matching cards successfully

201
00:08:34,856 --> 00:08:38,317
may have restrictions with mixed generations, vendors,

202
00:08:38,317 --> 00:08:41,178
or precision formats. Even when both GPUs

203
00:08:41,178 --> 00:08:43,293
are detected, that does not prove every

204
00:08:43,293 --> 00:08:45,280
model and split mode will be supported.

205
00:08:45,604 --> 00:08:47,515
So here's the decision test I would use

206
00:08:47,515 --> 00:08:50,712
before spending money. First, write down the exact

207
00:08:50,712 --> 00:08:54,775
GPU models, not just their VRAM capacities. Include

208
00:08:54,775 --> 00:08:58,520
the motherboard, processor, power supply, and operating system.

209
00:08:58,842 --> 00:09:02,484
Second, choose one actual model file and quantization.

210
00:09:02,484 --> 00:09:05,357
Decide the context length and number of simultaneous

211
00:09:05,357 --> 00:09:07,713
requests you care about. A setup that runs

212
00:09:07,713 --> 00:09:10,104
a short prompt is not necessarily a setup

213
00:09:10,104 --> 00:09:12,920
that handles a long document or multiple users.

214
00:09:13,253 --> 00:09:15,573
Third, test a single GPU as your

215
00:09:15,573 --> 00:09:18,842
baseline. Record the memory allocation, whether any

216
00:09:18,842 --> 00:09:20,862
layers are on the CPU, how long

217
00:09:20,862 --> 00:09:22,580
the prompt takes to process, and

218
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:24,430
how fast the response is generated.

219
00:09:24,750 --> 00:09:26,700
If you care about the user experience,

220
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:28,760
also record time to first token.

221
00:09:29,098 --> 00:09:32,559
Fourth, test a supported two-GPU configuration with that

222
00:09:32,559 --> 00:09:35,239
exact same workload. Check the runtime logs to

223
00:09:35,239 --> 00:09:37,547
see where the model weights and cache really

224
00:09:37,547 --> 00:09:39,951
live. Do not assume that both cards being

225
00:09:39,951 --> 00:09:42,290
visible means both are doing useful work.

226
00:09:42,643 --> 00:09:45,046
Fifth, compare the right outcome. For one

227
00:09:45,046 --> 00:09:48,220
interactive chat, look at responsiveness and generation

228
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:51,321
speed. For a server, measure completed requests

229
00:09:51,321 --> 00:09:55,022
and latency under realistic concurrency. For large-model

230
00:09:55,022 --> 00:09:57,871
access, ask whether the model runs reliably

231
00:09:57,871 --> 00:09:59,360
at the context length you need.

232
00:09:59,718 --> 00:10:02,519
Finally, repeat the measurements and check the quality

233
00:10:02,519 --> 00:10:04,983
of the actual answers. Do not treat one

234
00:10:04,983 --> 00:10:08,180
unusually fast run as your expected performance. And

235
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:10,283
do not confuse an increase in hardware

236
00:10:10,283 --> 00:10:13,070
utilization with an increase in useful work.

237
00:10:13,390 --> 00:10:16,950
If you're using llama.cpp, its documentation explains

238
00:10:16,950 --> 00:10:20,231
options for listing devices, selecting devices, choosing

239
00:10:20,231 --> 00:10:23,200
split modes, and controlling split proportions.

240
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:25,420
Don't blindly copy someone else's flags.

241
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:28,191
Those options and their limitations can change by

242
00:10:28,191 --> 00:10:31,773
version and hardware backend. One warning is especially

243
00:10:31,773 --> 00:10:35,439
worth remembering: forcing peer-to-peer behavior or unsupported

244
00:10:35,439 --> 00:10:37,771
advanced settings can cause problems rather than

245
00:10:37,771 --> 00:10:40,030
solving them. A configuration is not an

246
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:42,890
optimization just because the flag sounds powerful.

247
00:10:43,210 --> 00:10:45,064
Now we can finally compare the two buying

248
00:10:45,064 --> 00:10:48,886
choices. One thirty-two-gigabyte GPU gives you a large

249
00:10:48,886 --> 00:10:52,083
single-device memory budget. For a model and runtime

250
00:10:52,083 --> 00:10:53,994
that fit on it, that avoids the need

251
00:10:53,994 --> 00:10:56,320
to split work between two discrete cards.

252
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:58,543
It can be a simpler system to configure

253
00:10:58,543 --> 00:11:00,755
and troubleshoot. But that doesn't prove it will

254
00:11:00,755 --> 00:11:04,889
outperform every possible pair of sixteen-gigabyte cards. Actual

255
00:11:04,889 --> 00:11:07,966
processing speed depends on the exact GPUs,

256
00:11:07,966 --> 00:11:10,755
their memory bandwidth and compute, the software,

257
00:11:10,755 --> 00:11:13,190
the workload, and the chosen parallel strategy.

258
00:11:13,513 --> 00:11:16,253
Two sixteen-gigabyte GPUs can make sense when you

259
00:11:16,253 --> 00:11:18,741
already own one, have a suitable system for

260
00:11:18,741 --> 00:11:21,145
the second, and need more aggregate accelerator

261
00:11:21,145 --> 00:11:23,000
memory for a model that supports splitting.

262
00:11:23,320 --> 00:11:24,306
They can also be useful

263
00:11:24,306 --> 00:11:26,310
for processing independent requests.

264
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:28,429
But if your real problem is one slow

265
